@page "/example-form-mvvm"
@using HelmerDemo.BlazorServerTwo.Application.Features.ExampleForm.Domain
@using HelmerDemo.BlazorServerTwo.Application.Features.ExampleForm.Mvvm.Blazor
<h3>Decoupled persons form - MVVM-y</h3>

<p>MVVM applied to Blazor.</p>
<p>It is NOT MVVM, but it looks a bit like it. However, the ViewModel is not tied to one view, but multiple. I think this feels more natural to Blazor.</p>
<p>Advantages</p>
<ul>
    <li>Decoupling: Instead of the known Blazor examples, I will decouple. I still want changes in the Domain model, the Database model or blazor frontend not to affect one another.</li>
    <li>Flexibility: The architecture allows for more flexible changes and adaptations.</li>
</ul>
<p>Drawbacks</p>
<ul>
    <li>There is no synchronization between Blazor sessions. (Is that a drawback or an advantage?) You CAN add synchronization through notifications.</li>
    <li>There is a lot of code, due to all the adapters and contracts! This could be mistaken for complexity.</li>
    <li>Validation logic is both in Frontend and in Backend.</li>
    <li>Adding a property to a contract, means the FrontEnd does not break. There is no hard coupling. So how do I know what to fix in the Frontend?</li>
</ul>

@switch(ViewModel.State)
{
    case ViewModelState.Loading:
        <p>Loading...</p>
        break;
    case ViewModelState.Error:
        <p>Error: @ViewModel.ErrorMessage</p>
        break;
    case ViewModelState.Ready:
        <h4>People</h4>

        <p>List of all people entered:</p>
        <ol>
            @foreach(var person in ViewModel.People)
            {
                <li>@person.FullName</li>
            }
        </ol>

        <br />
        <h4>Add Person</h4>
        <EditForm Model="@ViewModel.AddPersonModel" OnValidSubmit="ViewModel.AddPersonAsync">
            <DataAnnotationsValidator />
            <ValidationSummary />
            <div>
                <label>First Name:</label>
                <InputText @bind-Value="ViewModel.AddPersonModel.FirstName" />
            </div>
            <div>
                <label>Last Name:</label>
                <InputText @bind-Value="ViewModel.AddPersonModel.LastName" />
            </div>
            <div>
                <label>Gender:</label>
                <InputSelect @bind-Value="ViewModel.AddPersonModel.Gender">
                    @foreach (var gender in Enum.GetValues(typeof(Gender)))
                    {
                        <option value="@gender">@gender</option>
                    }
                </InputSelect>
            </div>
            <button type="submit">Add Person</button>
        </EditForm>
        break;
}

